      "message": "### Inconsistent Bounds Checking (AES vs. SHA)\n\nIn `bootrom.cc`, you correctly check if the payload size will overflow the 32-bit `payload_len` field when wrapping a **SHA-256** command:\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e, BootromError\u003e\nBootromComm::SendBootromCommandSha(...) {\n  if (data.size() \u003e std::numeric_limits\u003cuint32_t\u003e::max()) {\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kPayloadSizeExceedsLimit);\n  }\n  // ...\n\n```\n\nHowever, you missed this exact same check in `SendBootromCommandAes`. It blindly casts `data.size()` to `uint32_t`, which would result in integer truncation if a massive payload were passed:\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e, BootromError\u003e\nBootromComm::SendBootromCommandAes(...) {\n  bootrom_protocol::SecureCommandAesHeader req_hdr;\n  req_hdr.algorithm \u003d crypto_type_;\n  req_hdr.payload_len \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(data.size())); // Missing check!\n\n```\n\n* **The Fix:** Copy the `std::numeric_limits\u003cuint32_t\u003e::max()` check to the top of `SendBootromCommandAes`.",
